  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 245 PM EST Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Above normal temperatures and tranquil weather \nthrough Saturday.\n\nNo major changes to the going forecast. With high pressure \nremaining overhead and the ridge aloft, expect clear skies \nthrough Friday. Daytime highs will be very pleasant with highs \nin the upper 60s/lower 70s while lows will be a seasonably cool \nupper 20s-mid 30s (warmest at the coast).\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Keeping a close eye on coastal low development \nthis weekend. \n\nAs an upper trough over the Gulf of America moves east, an area\nof low pressure will develop along a stalled frontal boundary \nover the northern Bahamas later Friday or Friday night. After \nthis, the models are very uncertain regarding the eventual \ndevelopment, track and speed of the low itself. In fact, the \n100+ member superensemble consisting of the EPS, GEFS and AI-EPS\nsuggest by 00z Monday the low being as far north and west as SE\nPA, to as far south and east as just southwest of Bermuda. \nHowever, the mean low track from each of the models suggest the \nAI-EPS being furthest west with the EPS being furthest east. \nGEFS trends over the past several runs have shown a consistent \nmean track about 50 miles east of Cape Hatteras. The eventual \ntrack of the low will be critical if any impacts occur over the \narea. Obviously the closer to the coast (or if it moves inland \nover NC), beneficial rain would spread across much of the area \nand there may be some gale force gusty SE-NE winds at the coast.\nMeanwhile, the further offshore track would confine any precip \nto areas immediately at the coast or even mostly offshore. With \nthe low not even developed yet leading to the continued \nuncertainty, the weekend forecast (especially late Saturday into\nSunday) is still one of very low confidence. Still will \nhighlight the best rainfall chances in areas east of I-95 and \nbring in some gustier E-NE winds closer to the coast.",
